// SL-227, the CROSS-MODULE row: a suspending recursive function reached through
// an import gets the same cycle rejection as one in the entry module.
//
// The callee is SPLICED into the entry closure (design 210 / SL-208), so a frame
// really is built for it and the cycle really would be embedded by value —
// `RecursionError` before the fix, like every other row here.
//
// THE CONTRAST THAT MATTERS is a cross-module callee the splice DECLINES to
// take: no frame is built for it, its cycle is never embedded, and it must keep
// compiling. That is blade's own dependency resolver — `visit` recurses into a
// dependency's dependencies and reaches the cooperative `Command.run`, so the
// cycle is in the suspending-call graph and reachable from blade's suspending
// `main`, while no `__Frame_visit` is ever built. SL-227's first attempt asked
// the cycle question of the raw graph and refused to compile the package
// manager; the `freestanding` lane caught it. The check now asks whether every
// frame on the cycle will actually be BUILT, which is the same question
// `_build_sub_frame` would have answered by diverging — so this file and a
// building blade are the two sides of one rule, and the blade side is gated by
// the `freestanding` and `bootstrap` lanes.
// (`{TESTDIR}` is this file's own directory, so the module dir — which lives
// beside the success tests — is reached with `..`.)
// COMPILE-FLAGS: --module-path recursion227={TESTDIR}/../modules/recursion227
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: suspending recursion is not allowed
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: descend -> descend

import std.task.{yield_now}
import recursion227.{descend}

func main() {
    yield_now()
    print("depth {descend(3)}")
}
